1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,820
This is one ten-dollar bill.

2
00:00:02,840 --> 00:00:05,620
By lunchtime, it's going to pay for four different

3
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:07,540
things, for four different people.

4
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:09,810
And nobody will lose a single cent.

5
00:00:10,109 --> 00:00:11,449
And on this tiny island,

6
00:00:11,469 --> 00:00:13,449
one loan is about to make the whole town

7
00:00:13,469 --> 00:00:14,249
richer...

8
00:00:14,269 --> 00:00:16,009
and then, almost broke.

9
00:00:16,029 --> 00:00:17,049
Same people.

10
00:00:17,069 --> 00:00:18,329
Same sandwiches.

11
00:00:18,349 --> 00:00:19,799
Same ten dollars.

12
00:00:20,045 --> 00:00:22,585
So let's figure out how money actually works.

13
00:00:22,605 --> 00:00:23,975
With one sandwich.

14
00:00:27,243 --> 00:00:28,983
Welcome to Crumb Island.

15
00:00:29,003 --> 00:00:31,063
Population: two hundred people,

16
00:00:31,083 --> 00:00:33,303
one bank, and the best sandwich shop in the

17
00:00:33,323 --> 00:00:34,173
ocean.

18
00:00:34,256 --> 00:00:35,036
This is Mo.

19
00:00:35,056 --> 00:00:36,556
Mo makes sandwiches.

20
00:00:36,576 --> 00:00:39,356
To make sandwiches, Mo needs bread from Bea.

21
00:00:39,376 --> 00:00:40,956
Bea wants fish from Finn.

22
00:00:40,976 --> 00:00:41,756
And Finn?

23
00:00:41,776 --> 00:00:43,466
Finn just wants a sandwich.

24
00:00:43,725 --> 00:00:45,145
MO: One sandwich, please...

25
00:00:45,165 --> 00:00:46,745
I mean, here's a sandwich!

26
00:00:46,765 --> 00:00:48,041
Can I have bread?

27
00:00:48,061 --> 00:00:49,241
BEE: I don't want a sandwich.

28
00:00:49,261 --> 00:00:50,292
I want fish.

29
00:00:50,312 --> 00:00:51,092
FINN: Don't look at me.

30
00:00:51,112 --> 00:00:52,242
I want a sandwich.

31
00:00:52,547 --> 00:00:55,087
Without money, everybody has to find someone who has

32
00:00:55,107 --> 00:00:57,247
what they need, and needs what they have,

33
00:00:57,267 --> 00:00:59,167
at the exact same moment.

34
00:00:59,187 --> 00:01:02,527
Economists call that the double coincidence of wants.

35
00:01:02,547 --> 00:01:04,447
Which is a fancy way of saying:

36
00:01:04,467 --> 00:01:05,247
good luck.

37
00:01:05,267 --> 00:01:06,447
And honestly?

38
00:01:06,467 --> 00:01:09,167
Historians have never found a town that really ran

39
00:01:09,187 --> 00:01:10,447
on pure barter.

40
00:01:10,467 --> 00:01:11,887
It's a thought experiment.

41
00:01:11,907 --> 00:01:14,077
But it shows why money is so handy.

42
00:01:14,387 --> 00:01:15,647
Money fixes it.

43
00:01:15,667 --> 00:01:18,447
Money is just something everyone agrees to accept.

44
00:01:18,467 --> 00:01:19,247
That's it.

45
00:01:19,267 --> 00:01:20,447
That's the whole trick.

46
00:01:20,467 --> 00:01:20,927
Everyone.

47
00:01:20,947 --> 00:01:21,797
Agrees.

48
00:01:22,761 --> 00:01:24,981
You see, money isn't really the paper.

49
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:26,261
It's the agreement.

50
00:01:26,281 --> 00:01:27,621
On the island of Yap,

51
00:01:27,641 --> 00:01:30,661
in the Pacific, people used giant stone wheels as

52
00:01:30,681 --> 00:01:31,301
money.

53
00:01:31,321 --> 00:01:34,261
A visitor in the early nineteen hundreds was told

54
00:01:34,281 --> 00:01:36,901
about one huge stone that sank in a storm

55
00:01:36,921 --> 00:01:38,101
on its way home,

56
00:01:38,121 --> 00:01:39,701
generations earlier...

57
00:01:39,721 --> 00:01:42,101
and the family who owned it was still considered

58
00:01:42,121 --> 00:01:42,971
rich.

59
00:01:43,044 --> 00:01:44,784
Nobody could see the stone.

60
00:01:44,804 --> 00:01:46,704
Everybody agreed it was there.

61
00:01:46,724 --> 00:01:49,424
So money is a story we all believe together.

62
00:01:49,444 --> 00:01:51,504
And like every story on this channel...

63
00:01:51,524 --> 00:01:52,734
it ripples.

64
00:01:53,695 --> 00:01:55,168
Watch the ten dollars.

65
00:01:55,188 --> 00:01:57,355
Ana, a nurse coming off a night shift,

66
00:01:57,375 --> 00:01:58,555
buys a sandwich.

67
00:01:58,575 --> 00:02:00,265
Ten dollars goes to Mo.

68
00:02:00,439 --> 00:02:01,569
ANA: Oh, I needed this.

69
00:02:02,107 --> 00:02:03,607
Mo pays Bea for bread.

70
00:02:03,627 --> 00:02:05,447
Bea pays Finn for fish.

71
00:02:05,467 --> 00:02:08,087
And Finn, starving after a morning at sea,

72
00:02:08,107 --> 00:02:09,797
walks into Mo's and buys...

73
00:02:09,947 --> 00:02:10,997
a sandwich.

74
00:02:11,054 --> 00:02:11,904
FINN: Finally.

75
00:02:12,236 --> 00:02:12,936
One bill.

76
00:02:12,956 --> 00:02:14,136
Four purchases.

77
00:02:14,156 --> 00:02:16,536
Forty dollars of stuff made and sold.

78
00:02:16,556 --> 00:02:19,016
And the bill is still worth ten dollars.

79
00:02:19,036 --> 00:02:21,336
Here's the part most people never hear:

80
00:02:21,356 --> 00:02:23,686
your spending is someone else's income.

81
00:02:23,922 --> 00:02:26,622
Ray Dalio, who built one of the biggest investment

82
00:02:26,642 --> 00:02:27,582
firms in the world,

83
00:02:27,602 --> 00:02:28,862
puts it this way:

84
00:02:28,882 --> 00:02:32,222
one person's spending is another person's income.

85
00:02:32,242 --> 00:02:35,262
Every dollar you spend lands in somebody's pocket.

86
00:02:35,282 --> 00:02:36,862
And every dollar you earn...

87
00:02:36,882 --> 00:02:38,332
came out of somebody's.

88
00:02:38,615 --> 00:02:40,755
Which raises a weird question.

89
00:02:40,775 --> 00:02:43,475
What happens when someone spends money that doesn't exist

90
00:02:43,495 --> 00:02:44,345
yet?

91
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:46,270
Mo has a dream.

92
00:02:46,290 --> 00:02:47,230
A second oven.

93
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:48,590
Twice the sandwiches.

94
00:02:48,610 --> 00:02:50,510
The oven costs a thousand dollars.

95
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:51,150
Mo has...

96
00:02:51,170 --> 00:02:52,380
sixty-two.

97
00:02:52,631 --> 00:02:54,691
So Mo goes to the Crumb Island Bank,

98
00:02:54,711 --> 00:02:56,001
to see Mister Penny.

99
00:02:56,131 --> 00:02:57,551
MR. PENNY: Reliable customer.

100
00:02:57,571 --> 00:02:59,071
Excellent sandwiches.

101
00:02:59,091 --> 00:03:00,061
Approved.

102
00:03:00,471 --> 00:03:02,958
Now, you probably think the bank takes a thousand

103
00:03:02,978 --> 00:03:04,851
dollars out of somebody's savings,

104
00:03:04,871 --> 00:03:06,451
and hands it to Mo.

105
00:03:06,471 --> 00:03:08,131
That's what most people think.

106
00:03:08,151 --> 00:03:09,521
It's not what happens.

107
00:03:09,788 --> 00:03:13,288
In 2014, the Bank of England explained it in

108
00:03:13,308 --> 00:03:16,168
plain words: whenever a bank makes a loan,

109
00:03:16,188 --> 00:03:18,328
it creates a matching deposit.

110
00:03:18,348 --> 00:03:22,088
Mister Penny types a thousand dollars into Mo's account...

111
00:03:22,108 --> 00:03:24,968
and a thousand new dollars exist that didn't exist

112
00:03:24,988 --> 00:03:26,038
a second ago.

113
00:03:26,116 --> 00:03:26,576
MO: Wait.

114
00:03:26,596 --> 00:03:27,056
You just...

115
00:03:27,076 --> 00:03:28,046
typed it?

116
00:03:28,229 --> 00:03:29,409
MR. PENNY: And you'll pay it back.

117
00:03:29,429 --> 00:03:30,559
With interest.

118
00:03:30,990 --> 00:03:33,690
In Britain, about ninety-seven percent of money is bank

119
00:03:33,710 --> 00:03:35,290
deposits, not cash.

120
00:03:35,310 --> 00:03:37,610
And most of that starts life as a loan.

121
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:38,730
Not printed.

122
00:03:38,750 --> 00:03:39,610
Lent.

123
00:03:39,630 --> 00:03:41,290
Banks can't do it forever:

124
00:03:41,310 --> 00:03:42,970
they're held back by profits,

125
00:03:42,990 --> 00:03:44,890
by rules, and by interest rates.

126
00:03:44,910 --> 00:03:46,650
And when a loan gets paid back,

127
00:03:46,670 --> 00:03:48,490
that money disappears again.

128
00:03:48,510 --> 00:03:50,250
Borrowing creates money.

129
00:03:50,270 --> 00:03:51,640
Repaying deletes it.

130
00:03:52,713 --> 00:03:54,053
Now watch the ripple.

131
00:03:54,073 --> 00:03:56,533
Mo pays Kai to build the oven in.

132
00:03:56,553 --> 00:03:58,773
Kai has money he didn't have yesterday,

133
00:03:58,793 --> 00:04:00,053
so he buys new boots,

134
00:04:00,073 --> 00:04:02,083
and takes his family out for crab.

135
00:04:02,186 --> 00:04:04,246
The Crab Shack is so busy it hires a

136
00:04:04,266 --> 00:04:05,206
new waiter.

137
00:04:05,226 --> 00:04:07,206
The waiter buys sandwiches.

138
00:04:07,226 --> 00:04:09,846
Mo sells more, so Mo buys more bread from

139
00:04:09,866 --> 00:04:12,516
Bea, and Bea buys more fish from Finn.

140
00:04:12,641 --> 00:04:15,101
One loan, and the whole town gets busier.

141
00:04:15,121 --> 00:04:16,381
Incomes go up.

142
00:04:16,401 --> 00:04:17,661
People feel richer.

143
00:04:17,681 --> 00:04:18,061
So...

144
00:04:18,081 --> 00:04:19,291
they borrow too.

145
00:04:19,490 --> 00:04:21,470
Bea borrows for a bigger bakery.

146
00:04:21,490 --> 00:04:23,790
Finn borrows for a faster boat.

147
00:04:23,810 --> 00:04:26,990
Everybody's spending more, so everybody's earning more.

148
00:04:27,010 --> 00:04:28,700
Crumb Island is booming.

149
00:04:28,935 --> 00:04:31,155
But there's a catch hiding in the party.

150
00:04:31,175 --> 00:04:34,355
When everyone has more money chasing the same sandwiches,

151
00:04:34,375 --> 00:04:35,635
prices creep up.

152
00:04:35,655 --> 00:04:38,275
The ten-dollar sandwich becomes twelve.

153
00:04:38,295 --> 00:04:39,585
Then fourteen.

154
00:04:39,831 --> 00:04:42,051
And every loan comes with a date on it.

155
00:04:42,071 --> 00:04:43,841
The day you have to pay it back.

156
00:04:45,493 --> 00:04:46,513
Repayment day.

157
00:04:46,533 --> 00:04:47,313
To pay the bank,

158
00:04:47,333 --> 00:04:48,753
Mo has to spend less.

159
00:04:48,773 --> 00:04:49,793
So does Bea.

160
00:04:49,813 --> 00:04:50,833
So does Finn.

161
00:04:50,853 --> 00:04:52,993
And all that money flows back to the bank...

162
00:04:53,013 --> 00:04:54,303
and disappears.

163
00:04:54,554 --> 00:04:58,374
But remember: one person's spending is another person's income.

164
00:04:58,394 --> 00:05:01,014
Mo spends less, so Kai earns less.

165
00:05:01,034 --> 00:05:02,534
Kai skips the crab.

166
00:05:02,554 --> 00:05:05,014
The Crab Shack lets the new waiter go.

167
00:05:05,034 --> 00:05:07,444
And the waiter stops buying sandwiches.

168
00:05:07,733 --> 00:05:09,393
The ripple runs backwards.

169
00:05:09,413 --> 00:05:11,233
And here's the strange part.

170
00:05:11,253 --> 00:05:13,713
Every single person is being sensible.

171
00:05:13,733 --> 00:05:15,233
Saving is smart!

172
00:05:15,253 --> 00:05:17,633
But when everybody tries to save at the same

173
00:05:17,653 --> 00:05:20,433
time, the whole town's income shrinks.

174
00:05:20,453 --> 00:05:23,183
Economists call it the paradox of thrift.

175
00:05:23,407 --> 00:05:26,027
Dalio calls the whole loop a debt cycle.

176
00:05:26,047 --> 00:05:27,787
Borrow, boom, pay back,

177
00:05:27,807 --> 00:05:28,507
bust.

178
00:05:28,527 --> 00:05:31,067
By his estimate, the short ones come around every

179
00:05:31,087 --> 00:05:32,507
five to eight years.

180
00:05:32,527 --> 00:05:34,107
There's no single villain.

181
00:05:34,127 --> 00:05:35,067
It's all of us,

182
00:05:35,087 --> 00:05:36,377
reacting to each other.

183
00:05:38,050 --> 00:05:39,710
Which brings us back to Ana.

184
00:05:39,730 --> 00:05:41,150
Her hours got cut.

185
00:05:41,170 --> 00:05:42,430
The rent is due.

186
00:05:42,450 --> 00:05:45,340
And her brain is doing something you might recognize.

187
00:05:45,597 --> 00:05:48,057
In one famous 2013 study,

188
00:05:48,077 --> 00:05:49,577
just thinking about a big,

189
00:05:49,597 --> 00:05:52,217
expensive car repair seemed to make people with less

190
00:05:52,237 --> 00:05:54,697
money do worse on reasoning puzzles.

191
00:05:54,717 --> 00:05:56,777
Like the worry was taking up space in their

192
00:05:56,797 --> 00:05:57,417
head.

193
00:05:57,437 --> 00:06:00,457
Fair warning: later attempts to repeat it have had

194
00:06:00,477 --> 00:06:04,057
mixed results, so scientists still disagree about how big

195
00:06:04,077 --> 00:06:05,447
that effect really is.

196
00:06:05,730 --> 00:06:08,270
But anyone who's been broke knows the feeling.

197
00:06:08,290 --> 00:06:09,950
The worry gets loud.

198
00:06:09,970 --> 00:06:11,790
Everything else gets quiet.

199
00:06:11,810 --> 00:06:13,550
And you're far from alone:

200
00:06:13,570 --> 00:06:18,190
in a 2026 survey by the American Psychological Association,

201
00:06:18,210 --> 00:06:21,550
seventy-one percent of American adults said money is a

202
00:06:21,570 --> 00:06:23,260
significant source of stress.

203
00:06:23,717 --> 00:06:25,697
So let's look at the same moment through two

204
00:06:25,717 --> 00:06:26,567
lenses.

205
00:06:26,841 --> 00:06:28,261
ANA: I'm terrible with money.

206
00:06:28,281 --> 00:06:29,971
I always mess everything up.

207
00:06:30,206 --> 00:06:31,386
Through the red lens,

208
00:06:31,406 --> 00:06:34,186
a town-wide slump becomes a personal failure.

209
00:06:34,206 --> 00:06:35,656
Through the gold lens...

210
00:06:35,754 --> 00:06:37,494
ANA: The whole island is struggling.

211
00:06:37,514 --> 00:06:38,774
This isn't just me.

212
00:06:38,794 --> 00:06:39,334
Okay.

213
00:06:39,354 --> 00:06:40,884
What can I actually do?

214
00:06:41,289 --> 00:06:42,869
First, the honest part:

215
00:06:42,889 --> 00:06:44,469
money stress is real.

216
00:06:44,489 --> 00:06:46,149
It isn't a mindset problem,

217
00:06:46,169 --> 00:06:49,109
and you can't positive-think your way out of rent.

218
00:06:49,129 --> 00:06:51,699
Real problems deserve real action.

219
00:06:52,093 --> 00:06:55,033
So psychologists suggest a two-question check.

220
00:06:55,053 --> 00:06:57,273
Question one: can I change this?

221
00:06:57,293 --> 00:06:58,713
If yes, change it.

222
00:06:58,733 --> 00:06:59,753
Make the call.

223
00:06:59,773 --> 00:07:01,273
Ask for a payment plan.

224
00:07:01,293 --> 00:07:02,823
Take one small step.

225
00:07:02,874 --> 00:07:03,974
MR. PENNY: A payment plan?

226
00:07:03,994 --> 00:07:04,854
Absolutely.

227
00:07:04,874 --> 00:07:06,244
Let's sort it out.

228
00:07:06,538 --> 00:07:09,398
Question two: if I can't change it right now,

229
00:07:09,418 --> 00:07:11,318
can I change how I see it?

230
00:07:11,338 --> 00:07:14,678
In one 2013 study of a hundred and seventy

231
00:07:14,698 --> 00:07:17,718
people, being good at reframing was linked to less

232
00:07:17,738 --> 00:07:20,518
depression when the stress was out of their control.

233
00:07:20,538 --> 00:07:22,838
But when they could have changed the situation,

234
00:07:22,858 --> 00:07:25,108
reframing alone was linked to more.

235
00:07:25,413 --> 00:07:27,313
So the trick isn't think positive.

236
00:07:27,333 --> 00:07:28,913
It's: change what you can,

237
00:07:28,933 --> 00:07:30,273
reframe what you can't.

238
00:07:30,293 --> 00:07:32,223
And notice which one you're facing.

239
00:07:33,669 --> 00:07:34,769
Back on Crumb Island,

240
00:07:34,789 --> 00:07:36,529
the slump doesn't last forever.

241
00:07:36,549 --> 00:07:38,129
Debts get paid down.

242
00:07:38,149 --> 00:07:39,249
Prices settle.

243
00:07:39,269 --> 00:07:39,969
And then...

244
00:07:39,989 --> 00:07:41,519
somebody goes first.

245
00:07:41,770 --> 00:07:44,230
Mo buys a loaf from Bea he doesn't strictly

246
00:07:44,250 --> 00:07:44,710
need.

247
00:07:44,730 --> 00:07:46,150
Bea buys a fish.

248
00:07:46,170 --> 00:07:47,830
Finn buys a sandwich.

249
00:07:47,850 --> 00:07:50,100
And the ripple starts turning forward again.

250
00:07:50,405 --> 00:07:53,265
Because an economy isn't some machine far away.

251
00:07:53,285 --> 00:07:55,825
It's every sandwich, every paycheck,

252
00:07:55,845 --> 00:07:57,185
every yes from a bank,

253
00:07:57,205 --> 00:08:00,305
and every person deciding to spend or to save.

254
00:08:00,325 --> 00:08:02,655
All of us, rippling into each other.

255
00:08:03,022 --> 00:08:05,482
Your ten dollars is somebody's income.

256
00:08:05,502 --> 00:08:07,642
Where you spend it is a tiny vote for

257
00:08:07,662 --> 00:08:09,002
what your town looks like.

258
00:08:09,022 --> 00:08:11,002
And when money stress gets loud,

259
00:08:11,022 --> 00:08:12,682
ask the two questions.

260
00:08:12,702 --> 00:08:13,882
Can I change it?

261
00:08:13,902 --> 00:08:15,512
Or can I reframe it?

262
00:08:15,874 --> 00:08:17,694
Quick question for the comments.

263
00:08:17,714 --> 00:08:19,694
If the whole island got scared at once,

264
00:08:19,714 --> 00:08:22,254
would you: A, save every coin you've got?

265
00:08:22,274 --> 00:08:24,334
Or B, keep buying sandwiches?

266
00:08:24,354 --> 00:08:25,854
There's no wrong answer.

267
00:08:25,874 --> 00:08:27,644
But the island needs both.

268
00:08:28,122 --> 00:08:29,542
Now if you'll excuse me...

269
00:08:29,562 --> 00:08:30,762
I'm getting a sandwich.
